TerraTec DMX 6Fire USB installation guide
=========================================

Safety warning
--------------

The stock kernel module is named "snd-usb-6fire". On the hardware-tested
kernel listed in README.md, that module caused a kernel panic while initializing
the device. The fixed out-of-tree module is deliberately named
"snd-usb-6fire-fixed" so the stock module can be blacklisted independently.
Module-management tools display their internal names with underscores
("snd_usb_6fire" and "snd_usb_6fire_fixed"); kmod treats hyphens and
underscores as equivalent.

Disconnect the DMX 6Fire USB before starting. Do not reconnect it until the
fixed module and the stock-module blacklist have been verified.

The commands below assume they are run from the root of this source checkout.
The tested distribution is Nobara Linux 43. Package names and initramfs commands
may differ on other distributions.


Prerequisites on Nobara and Fedora
----------------------------------

Install DKMS, the build tools, headers for the running kernel, firmware-script
dependencies, and the Secure Boot utility:

  $ sudo dnf install dkms gcc make kernel-devel-$(uname -r) curl rpm cpio mokutil

Confirm that the kernel build directory is available:

  $ test -e /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build && echo "kernel headers found"

On other distributions, install equivalent packages providing DKMS, a C
compiler, make, the headers for the running kernel, curl, rpm2cpio, cpio, and
mokutil.


Install the device firmware
---------------------------

The hardware requires proprietary firmware. The supplied script downloads and
installs it under "/lib/firmware/6fire":

  $ chmod +x get_firmware.sh
  $ ./get_firmware.sh

Verify that the three firmware files are present:

  $ ls -l /lib/firmware/6fire/dmx6firel2.ihx \
      /lib/firmware/6fire/dmx6fireap.ihx \
      /lib/firmware/6fire/dmx6firecf.bin

Keep the device disconnected after installing the firmware.


Protect the system from the stock module
----------------------------------------

Blacklist only the stock module. The fixed module has a different name and is
not affected by this rule:

  $ printf '%s\n' 'blacklist snd_usb_6fire' | \
      sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-6fire.conf

On dracut-based distributions, also prevent the stock module from being copied
into an initramfs:

  $ printf '%s\n' 'omit_drivers+=" snd_usb_6fire snd-usb-6fire "' | \
      sudo tee /etc/dracut.conf.d/99-omit-6fire.conf

Do not use "blacklist snd_usb_6fire_fixed".


Install the fixed module with DKMS
----------------------------------

DKMS requires a versioned source directory. Install only the source and
metadata needed for building; this avoids copying Git data and local build
artifacts:

  $ sudo install -d -m 0755 /usr/src/sixfireusb-1.0
  $ sudo cp -a Makefile dkms.conf LICENSE CHANGES README.md \
      chip.c comm.c control.c firmware.c midi.c pcm.c substream.c urbs.c \
      chip.h comm.h common.h control.h firmware.h midi.h pcm.h rates.h \
      substream.h urbs.h /usr/src/sixfireusb-1.0/

Register, build, and install the module for the running kernel:

  $ sudo dkms add -m sixfireusb -v 1.0
  $ sudo dkms build -m sixfireusb -v 1.0 -k $(uname -r)
  $ sudo dkms install -m sixfireusb -v 1.0 -k $(uname -r)
  $ sudo depmod -a

DKMS installs the module as "snd-usb-6fire-fixed". Fedora-family systems
normally place DKMS modules under "/lib/modules/<kernel>/extra"; some other
distributions use "/lib/modules/<kernel>/updates/dkms".

Rebuild initramfs files on Nobara and Fedora after both DKMS installation and
blacklist configuration:

  $ sudo dracut --regenerate-all --force

Use the equivalent initramfs update command on other distributions.


Secure Boot
-----------

Check whether Secure Boot is enabled:

  $ mokutil --sb-state

DKMS normally signs its module with the certificate configured in
"/etc/dkms/framework.conf", commonly "/var/lib/dkms/mok.pub". Check whether
that certificate is enrolled:

  $ sudo mokutil --test-key /var/lib/dkms/mok.pub

If Secure Boot is enabled and the key is not enrolled, request enrollment:

  $ sudo mokutil --import /var/lib/dkms/mok.pub

Choose a temporary password, reboot, and complete MOK enrollment in the firmware
screen. Do not reconnect the DMX 6Fire until enrollment is complete.

After booting, the module signer can be inspected with:

  $ modinfo -F signer snd_usb_6fire_fixed


Verify before connecting the device
-----------------------------------

Confirm DKMS installation and the resolved module path:

  $ dkms status -m sixfireusb
  $ modinfo -n snd_usb_6fire_fixed
  $ modinfo -F vermagic snd_usb_6fire_fixed

The module path must end in "snd-usb-6fire-fixed.ko" with an optional
compression suffix. It must not resolve to "snd-usb-6fire.ko".

Confirm that the stock blacklist is active:

  $ modprobe -c | grep '^blacklist snd_usb_6fire$'

Simulate the udev modalias request. The output must contain
"snd-usb-6fire-fixed" and must not insert "snd-usb-6fire":

  $ modprobe -b -n -v \
      'usb:v0CCDp0080d0000dc00dsc00dp00ic00isc00ip00in00'

Load the fixed module explicitly:

  $ sudo modprobe snd_usb_6fire_fixed
  $ lsmod | grep '^snd_usb_6fire_fixed'

Reconnect the DMX 6Fire USB only after these checks pass. Verify ALSA detection:

  $ aplay -l

Kernel messages for the current boot can be inspected with:

  $ journalctl -b -k | grep -i 6fire


Migrating from the old my-6fire/1.0 DKMS setup
----------------------------------------------

Keep the device disconnected throughout migration. Install and verify the new
"sixfireusb/1.0" package using the preceding instructions before removing the
old package.

If the old module is loaded, unload it:

  $ sudo modprobe -r snd_usb_6fire

Remove the old DKMS registration:

  $ sudo dkms remove -m my-6fire -v 1.0 --all

The earlier setup may also have left a manually installed
"snd-usb-6fire.ko" under an "updates" or "extra" directory. Locate the module
that modprobe would currently select:

  $ modinfo -n snd_usb_6fire

If that command reports a non-kernel path such as
"/lib/modules/<kernel>/updates/snd-usb-6fire.ko.zst", move that exact file to a
backup outside "/lib/modules" and then refresh module metadata. Do not remove
the distribution-owned file under "/lib/modules/<kernel>/kernel":

  $ sudo install -d /var/lib/sixfireusb-backup
  $ sudo mv /the/exact/non-kernel/path/from-modinfo \
      /var/lib/sixfireusb-backup/
  $ sudo depmod -a
  $ sudo dracut --regenerate-all --force

The stock blacklist must remain in place after migration.


After a kernel update
---------------------

DKMS should build the fixed module automatically when a new kernel is
installed. Before connecting the device or selecting the new kernel as the
normal boot target, verify:

  $ dkms status -m sixfireusb
  $ modinfo -n snd_usb_6fire_fixed
  $ modprobe -b -n -v \
      'usb:v0CCDp0080d0000dc00dsc00dp00ic00isc00ip00in00'

If the new kernel is not listed as installed, keep the device disconnected and
run:

  $ sudo dkms autoinstall -k $(uname -r)
  $ sudo depmod -a
  $ sudo dracut --regenerate-all --force

Do not remove the stock blacklist merely because a DKMS build failed. Fix the
build or boot a previously verified kernel.


Updating this driver
--------------------

Do not overwrite an active DKMS source tree and assume DKMS will rebuild it.
For a new release, update PACKAGE_VERSION in "dkms.conf", install the source
under the matching "/usr/src/sixfireusb-<version>" directory, and register that
new version. Remove the previous DKMS version only after the new one has built
and passed the verification steps.


Uninstalling
------------

Disconnect the device and unload the fixed module:

  $ sudo modprobe -r snd_usb_6fire_fixed

Remove the DKMS package:

  $ sudo dkms remove -m sixfireusb -v 1.0 --all
  $ sudo depmod -a
  $ sudo dracut --regenerate-all --force

The source directory "/usr/src/sixfireusb-1.0" can then be archived or removed.

For safety, uninstalling the fixed module does not imply that the stock driver
is safe. Leave "/etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-6fire.conf" and
"/etc/dracut.conf.d/99-omit-6fire.conf" in place unless intentionally restoring
the stock driver and accepting its known risk.


Troubleshooting
---------------

If "modprobe snd_usb_6fire_fixed" reports "Required key not available", complete
the Secure Boot MOK enrollment described above.

If the module is missing for the running kernel, inspect:

  $ dkms status
  $ sudo dkms build -m sixfireusb -v 1.0 -k $(uname -r)
  $ find /var/lib/dkms/sixfireusb -name make.log -print

If the USB alias does not resolve to the fixed module, refresh module metadata:

  $ sudo depmod -a

If the device was already connected while installing, disconnect it, confirm
that "snd_usb_6fire_fixed" is loaded, and reconnect it.


Manual installation without DKMS
--------------------------------

Manual installation is intended only for development and does not survive
kernel updates automatically:

  $ make
  $ sudo make install
  $ sudo depmod -a

The manually built module may also be unsigned. Do not combine manual
"make install" with DKMS installation for the same kernel.
